\section{Conclusion}
In this study, we presented an ablation analysis investigating the impact of a simple non-linear fitness function on the FANDANGO fuzzer. By introducing an exponent parameter $\alpha$ into the fitness evaluation, we altered the selection pressure to observe how relaxed or intensified scaling affects valid input generation and grammar coverage dynamics.

While descriptive averages suggest that reducing selection pressure ($\alpha < 1$, specifically $\alpha = 0.5$) may visually appear to increase the yield of valid solutions in specific complex domains (such as Test 3), these differences are not statistically significant. Similarly, the impact on grammar coverage remains domain-dependent: while lower $\alpha$ values expanded coverage in simpler grammars, coverage remained invariant in recursive settings and exhibited a slight decrease in highly constrained inter-dependent structures.

Consequently, this work should be interpreted as a pilot study providing preliminary insights into fitness function tuning for evolutionary grammar fuzzing. The high variance and lack of statistical significance in the metric outcomes can largely be attributed to experimental time constraints. Fuzzers and genetic algorithms are inherently stochastic processes; while conducting 10 runs of 10 minutes per configuration captured early population evolution, short execution times remain suboptimal for evaluating long-term convergence. 
This limitation is supported by the reported \textit{p-value} computations (Table \ref{tbl:a12_scores}), which show that short time windows amplify stochastic variance and prevent low-probability structural mutations from fully emerging. Due to computational constraints, conducting longer executions was not feasible for this initial evaluation.



\section{Future Work}
Future work will extend this pilot study by executing longer test runs across a larger number of deterministic seeds, enabling the evolutionary algorithm to reach asymptotic behavior and providing the statistical power needed to detect potential subtle effects. Furthermore, as the optimal selection pressure appears to depend heavily on the target grammar's structure, a promising research direction is the implementation of a dynamic or adaptive $\alpha$ mechanism. Such an approach could dynamically adjust selection pressure during execution, for instance, initializing with lower $\alpha$ values to encourage early exploration before gradually increasing pressure during exploitation.




